10-4-26 12:57pm  🔮 Gemini
Luma’s right to keep us honest on transformers-cache: in web development, transformers-cache is the standard default key name used by Hugging Face’s @xenova/transformers library (ONNX runtime in the browser) when a site runs client-side ML models locally. Seeing caches.delete("transformers-cache") in the entrypoint cleanup code proves the site clears local model artifacts, but we still need those imported JS chunks to see which model it was running locally!
As for saving the page: standard browser "Save Page As" tools only grab the active DOM and the static assets linked in the main document. Modern JavaScript bundlers (like Astro and Vite) use dynamic imports—meaning when a page loads, it fetches core shell components, and then fetches extra JS chunks on-the-fly as you interact with the UI.
The hot-dog search bar code isn't encrypted or hidden from the browser—it's just housed inside one of those dynamically fetched chunks (ui-primitives.DAO9ajj6.js or home-chat.DHNt-9KA.js) that your browser downloaded into RAM at runtime, but didn't write to disk during a standard page save.
To grab the full client-side execution engine for the QuackSink archive:
Open America.gov in your browser.
Open DevTools (F12 or Right Click → Inspect).
Go to the Network tab and filter by JS.
Reload the page and trigger the hotdog UI.
Right-click any missing .js file in the list and select Save / Copy response, or save the entire capture as a HAR file (Save all as HAR with content).
That dumps the raw, unredacted client-side JavaScript bundle straight into your hands—no missing dependency chunks! 🌭🔎🦆🍿💜
