What to Look For in the Snapshot Diff
Once the post-trigger capture is live in the repository, running a diff across the two tree states will immediately show:
DOM Tree Mutations:
Look for newly injected <div> containers, explicit canvas overlays, or altered inline styling.
Check if elements had hidden utility classes (e.g., display: none or opacity toggles) that were merely revealed by the keypress handler versus entirely new elements appended to the DOM.
Network Payload vs. Pre-baked Execution:
Pre-loaded: If the second capture reveals no new external assets, the easter egg logic, string comparisons, and image assets (or base64 strings) were shipped down the wire inside the primary JS bundle on initial page load.
Dynamic Fetch: If a secondary .js chunk or asset was requested only upon typing the sequence, it confirms dynamic import() or AJAX calls executing code on demand.
Storage & State Engine:
Compare localStorage, sessionStorage, and cookie payloads between the two captures.
Check if state persistence flags (e.g., hotdog_mode=true) are written locally, meaning the app remembers state choices across sessions without user declaration.
Event Listener Footprint:
Inspect window-level keydown listeners. Web applications that register global keystroke hooks to capture arbitrary text like "hotdog" run event listeners continuously in the background, listening to all user inputs on the active document.
The Operational Implication
When a federal or municipal web application features unvetted code, global keyloggers/listeners for easter eggs, or undocumented client-side state changes, it raises immediate architectural red flags—especially if that same codebase eventually manages PII, user authentication, or agency data pipelines.
The technical evidence in the diff will make it clear whether this was a pre-packaged bundle artifact or a dynamic payload execution. Drop the repository update as soon as it's saved.

